62 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050954 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186073684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050954 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186073685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050954 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186073686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050954 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186073687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050954 #edb/ref 1031 "Multiplying Fractions by Unit Fractions Using Models" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050954 #edb/ref 1033 565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050954 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "75482856-dc6f-5409-9718-62a9c17846a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161716 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14520" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161716 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/01/0144d5fb5264162b7b2f03f71469f261c33c813ee235743c9e631edd7d4d049c.png)\n\nWhat multiplication problem is represented by the model above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161716 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "432754ac-d9f0-5843-a751-b13821e2e725" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161716 #edb/ref 1074 "We have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\non the left and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{20}\n$$\n\non the right.\n\n![](images/f0/f087c076a86b5bab709af23db87d63ed66363d4d580c23dc453573f264fb28bc.png)\n\nTherefore, the multiplication problem shown is:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{5} \\times \\dfrac{3}{4} = \\dfrac{6}{20}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161717 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14523" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161717 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/23/23940bc011c6cd71c0d9d58b4bab0b175722a21fd1c80062ed5cb5ceff3729c0.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the multiplication problem above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161717 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1fbd22af-5d6d-58ff-ac16-cfbb1d2577de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161717 #edb/ref 1074 "We have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\non the left and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{20}\n$$\n\non the right.\n\n![](images/31/31badccda3cc3548e2bdc40158e88d32feddcf92b8fdd4d4ae18f08396ca8cfc.png)\n\nThe shape on the right-hand side is split into $5$ equal parts by horizontal lines.\nSo, the shaded part on the right is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{5}\n$$\n\nof the shaded part on the left.\n\nTherefore, the multiplication problem shown is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4} \\times \\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{5} = \\dfrac{9}{20}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $\\color{blue}3$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161718 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14528" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161718 #edb/ref 1071 "What picture is missing from the multiplication model below?\n\n![](images/0d/0d381d096814ced0d6637ce373e14d77f317e1d8f202ed75371f744804d99f3c.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161718 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "493da564-eed4-5ec7-b527-63fd3a68fc95" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161718 #edb/ref 1074 "In the model, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\non the left.\n\nSince we need to multiply by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}4}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\n, we split the whole into $\\color{blue}5$ equal parts horizontally and ensure that $\\color{red}4$ of the parts remain shaded.\n\n![](images/97/978789f7aed1ea0e0dc8794aaeb00a3829168101e792129e72c0585fac43ebe2.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we see that the shaded part represents\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{15}\n$$\n\nof the whole.\n\nTherefore, the missing picture is as follows:\n\n![](images/fa/faf527aba9974aa23b9a5bffd8fc8f54a2135fce10bf2d09a7133556ab4b95e3.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161719 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14539" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161719 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/60/6026c90a1101bf484ab649dcb35964ae4b136fc7784e384d1cf5d90ea47a96a2.png)\n\nUse the model above to calculate $\\dfrac{2}{6} \\times \\dfrac{3}{4}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161719 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2e14de76-f6e2-51f8-9590-8283fe81ce82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161719 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we need to multiply by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\n, we split the whole into $\\color{blue}4$ equal parts horizontally and ensure that $\\color{red}3$ of the parts remain shaded.\n\n![](images/0f/0f7c5ff7155c00a811c744606ee039e764c6ded16f214a163b6d5d4a481e633b.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we see that the shaded part represents\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{24}\n$$\n\nof the whole.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{6} \\times \\dfrac{3}{4} = \\dfrac{6}{24}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161913 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161913 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161913 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161913 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying a Multiplication Problem Corresponding to a Model" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161913 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161913 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3443d2b1-14c5-50ba-90b3-73b8bef47016" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161914 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161914 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161914 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying a Missing Number" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161914 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161914 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "d06ad10a-4be6-54c4-94b2-17a25f0c2c16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161915 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161915 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161915 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying a Fraction Model Representing a Product of Two Fractions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161915 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161915 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "905c4fc9-5e62-5757-bee9-a9b277b62278" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161916 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161916 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161916 #edb/ref 1039 "Multiplying Two Fractions Using Fraction Models" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161916 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161916 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "fa21548a-67bc-506c-8d6f-980aec5950dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162206 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162206 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162206 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162206 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162206 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162206 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162206 #edb/ref 1045 "Multiplying Fractions Using Models" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162206 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186162729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162206 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162206 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "851263af-22ca-51b7-b65b-78ee6f9ebb50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162729 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162729 #edb/ref 1061 135386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162729 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a78e7754-a22c-59f9-9aa8-121a958efa96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162729 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162730 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162730 #edb/ref 1061 135387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162730 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ce6efcc9-d64a-577b-b5c0-6b41eb1de5ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162730 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186161913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162731 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162731 #edb/ref 1061 135391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162731 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "89847e6c-e242-532d-83bb-f8a0289e83e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162731 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186161914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162732 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162732 #edb/ref 1061 135395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162732 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3b3bbfe0-8570-53c6-a5b8-1b924bcf020b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162732 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186161915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162733 #edb/ref 1061 135399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162733 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a9b790a0-3cdf-5858-9145-025944236be0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162733 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186161916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163366 #edb/ref 1064 "We can use fraction models to multiply any two fractions.\n\nLet's figure out how to use models to compute the following product:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{5} \\times \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nWe start with the model of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\n![](images/06/06869533fb9eae08e88187c0965c9683c3b00e8cf269b121f416ce7f473ad508.png)\n\nSince we need to multiply by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\n, we split the whole into $\\color{blue}3$ equal parts *horizontally*. But this time, we keep the shaded pieces in the bottom $\\color{red}2$ rows.\n\n![](images/0c/0c449cdff6dabfd6599925b0d450ab2d579ce49d2729b9a98fe63bb8992a850d.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we see that the shaded part represents\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{15}\n$$\n\nof the whole.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{5} \\times \\dfrac{2}{3} = \\dfrac{6}{15}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163366 #edb/ref 1065 8978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163366 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "98bc8936-f81c-50ef-a2f7-b9cb50262591" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163366 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
